      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 160C (Fan 140C / Gas 3). Brush the rectangular baking tin generously with melted butter."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the pistachios, walnuts and cinnamon together in a bowl. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lay one sheet of filo into the tin and brush all over with melted butter using the pastry brush. Repeat until you have 10 sheets layered up, brushing each one well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spread the nut mixture evenly over the layered filo."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Layer the remaining filo sheets on top, buttering each one as before. Use the digital scales to weigh out the correct amount of butter to ensure you use enough."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "With a sharp knife, cut the baklava into diamond or square shapes all the way through to the base. This is essential before baking as the pastry sets hard and cannot be cut cleanly afterwards."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bake for 35 to 40 minutes until golden all over."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "While the baklava bakes, make the syrup: combine the honey, caster sugar, lemon juice and 150ml water in the medium saucepan. Bring to the boil, stirring until the sugar dissolves, then simmer for 10 minutes. Remove from heat and stir in the rose water. Keep warm."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The moment the baklava comes out of the oven, pour the warm syrup evenly over the entire surface. Leave to soak at room temperature for at least 2 hours before serving."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Filo tears when layering"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Keep filo sheets under a damp tea towel while you work. They dry out and tear within minutes of exposure to air. Work quickly and do not worry about small tears; subsequent layers cover them."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Baklava is soggy not flaky"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The syrup must be poured over hot baklava and allowed to absorb completely before covering. Covering too early traps steam and softens the pastry. Leave it uncovered at room temperature."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Cuts ragged after baking"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Always cut before baking. Once baked and soaked, filo crumbles under a knife. Pre-cutting ensures clean, defined pieces and also allows the syrup to penetrate right into each portion."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Few desserts inspire as much regional pride as "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "baklava-term"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "baklava"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". The Turkish city of Gaziantep is officially recognised for its pistachio baklava and takes the recipe extremely seriously. Turkish baklava tends to use clarified butter and a lighter syrup than Greek versions, and is far less sweet than many people expect. It keeps well for several days at room temperature, improving as the syrup distributes through the layers."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}